    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "description",
      "value": "Design and execute a numerical experiment to test the hypothesis by scanning network width N for varying constraint counts w. Define rigid/fat families as distinct analytical constraint sets (e.g., low-rank algebraic vs. high-entropy random). Train small MLPs with width N, evaluate zero-violation (max abs error ≤ 1e-3) on held-out constraints, and record minimal N_c(w). Compare scaling: rigid should show N_c(w) ~ α log(w) + β; fat should exceed search limit (N > 999). Use symbolic regression to fit N_c(w) and validate against information-theoretic bounds."
    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "skill",
      "value": "None (custom ML experiment)"
    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "expected_prediction",
      "value": "For rigid family, N_c(w) will grow logarithmically with w, reaching ~10–50 for w up to 1000; for fat family, no width ≤ 999 achieves zero violation, so N_c(w) remains undefined (or exceeds limit). The scaling will distinguish probabilistic phase transition from constant saturation."
    },
