    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "reviewer_critique",
      "value": "Peer review:\n\n1) Missing convergence check:\n- The code varies `w` (number of training points), but there is no convergence study with respect to resolution (e.g., test set size, number of hidden units, or polynomial order). \n- No error-vs-sampling-rate plot or table to show whether the reported errors decrease monotonically as `w` increases, and at what rate (consistent with theory for the method used).\n\n2) Reproducibility and benchmarking:\n- The script is incomplete (truncated at `mlp_fit`), so the actual model architecture, optimizer, loss function, and stopping criteria are unspecified. This prevents reproduction.\n- No comparison to baselines (e.g., least-squares polynomial fit on the rigid case, or a standard regression method on the fat case). With only “passed: 0, failed: 0, skipped: 2” benchmarks, there is no evidence of correctness or improvement.\n- Uncertainty quantification is absent: no confidence intervals or multiple-seed statistics beyond what’s implied (seeds are used in generation but not aggregated/summarized).\n\n3) Domain/knowledge alignment:\n- The rigid case is a known quadratic polynomial; the reported errors (order 10^-3 to 10^-4) suggest approximation is possible, but without showing fitted coefficients or residuals, it’s unclear whether the method recovers the true structure or just fits noise.\n- The fat case uses a random 10-coefficient polynomial with many training points; achieving error ~2×10^-4 on one point (the fourth entry) while errors are 1.0 on the first three is suspicious and may indicate underfitting, data leakage, or a mismatch between training/test distributions.\n\n4) Concrete next steps:\n- Complete and document the full pipeline: model definition, hyperparameters, training procedure, and evaluation metric (e.g., RMSE/MAE on a held-out set).\n- Add a convergence study: fix the true function, vary `w` and report test error with standard error over multiple seeds; include a baseline (e.g., closed-form polynomial regression).\n- Report uncertainty: run multiple random seeds for training and report mean ± std of test errors; use cross-validation if data are scarce.\n- Add visualization: plot true vs. predicted curves for both rigid and fat cases on a fine grid, and optionally show residuals.\n- If the goal is symbolic discovery or structure recovery, include a check on recovered coefficients (for the rigid case) and compare against the ground truth."
